# 8. Step 7 — Preserve Evidence

I created an investigation directory:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/day20-ir/{system,logs,network,process,files}
```

Collected system information:

```bash
date > ~/day20-ir/system/date.txt
hostname > ~/day20-ir/system/hostname.txt
id > ~/day20-ir/system/id.txt
uptime > ~/day20-ir/system/uptime.txt
```

Collected process information:

```bash
ps aux > ~/day20-ir/process/processes.txt
```

Collected network connections:

```bash
sudo ss -tunap > ~/day20-ir/network/connections.txt
```

Collected logged-in users:

```bash
who > ~/day20-ir/system/who.txt
w > ~/day20-ir/system/w.txt
last > ~/day20-ir/system/last.txt
```

Collected SSH logs:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ssh --since today > ~/day20-ir/logs/ssh.log
```

On Ubuntu/Debian systems:

```bash
sudo cp /var/log/auth.log ~/day20-ir/logs/auth.log
```

### Why preserve evidence?

Evidence should be collected carefully so that the original information is not unnecessarily modified or destroyed.

I should **not immediately delete or modify a suspicious file** during an investigation.

---

# 9. Step 8 — Hash Evidence

I calculated the SHA-256 hash of collected evidence:

```bash
sha256sum ~/day20-ir/logs/auth.log
```

I can create hashes for multiple evidence files:

```bash
sha256sum ~/day20-ir/logs/* > ~/day20-ir/evidence-hashes.txt
```

Hashes can help determine whether collected evidence has changed.

